course: dictionary
lesson: 4760
title: The housefly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A housefly is a common small grey fly. It lives near people and buzzes around houses, food and rubbish.
explain: Like many insects, a housefly goes through a life cycle with four stages: egg, larva or maggot, pupa, and adult. Each stage is different, and the cycle then starts again.
explain: Houseflies matter for health. «A housefly tastes with its feet, spits juices to turn food to liquid and mops it up; it can carry germs from rubbish and dung, so we cover food.» Covering food keeps germs away from what we eat.
fact: A housefly is a true fly with only two wings.
fact: «A housefly tastes with its feet.»
fact: Houseflies can carry germs from rubbish and dung onto our food.
fact: «Its eyes see movement very fast, which is why it is so hard to swat.»
fact: The tiny knobs called halteres are the shrunken back pair of a true fly's wings.
exercise: Where does a housefly usually live? | *Near people, in houses | Under the sea | On high mountain tops | In deep caves
exercise: What can a housefly carry from rubbish onto food? | Feathers | Sunshine | Sand | *Germs
exercise: «True flies, like houseflies, hoverflies and mosquitoes, have only two wings.» How many wings does a housefly have? | Eight | Four | Six | *Two
test: Why do we cover food? | To make it look nicer to eat | To keep it cold | *To stop flies spreading germs onto it | To stop it getting dry in the sun
test: Why is a housefly so hard to swat? | *Its eyes see movement very fast | It is invisible to people | It is too big to hit | It can only fly backwards
exam: The back pair of wings of a true fly, like a housefly, has shrunk into tiny knobs called what? | Shells | Petals | *Halteres | Feathers
